    "code": "E1",
    "name": "Mediation enquiry, first response",
    "subject": "Your mediation enquiry, Brighter Future Mediation [{{case_ref}}]",
    "bodyHtml": "<p>Hello {{client_first_name}},</p><p><br></p><p>Many thanks for reaching out. {{personalised_response}}</p><p><br></p><p>The first step, in all instances, is to attend a Mediation Information and Assessment Meeting (MIAM). This is a confidential one-to-one online session lasting up to one hour with an FMC-registered mediator. It is your chance to:</p><p><br></p><p>discuss your situation and concerns in a safe, neutral space</p><p>understand all the options available to you</p><p>learn how mediation could help you reach arrangements that work for your family</p><p>ask questions and make an informed decision about next steps.</p><p><br></p><p>In most instances a MIAM is a legal requirement before applying to the family court, and it is mandatory to attempt negotiation before court proceedings. There are limited exemptions. You can find out more about MIAMs and mediation <a href=\"{{link_miam}}\">here</a>.</p><p><br></p><p>We do not provide legally aided mediation. If you receive certain benefits or are on a low income you may qualify for free mediation with a legal aid provider; you can check eligibility <a href=\"{{link_legalaid_check}}\">here</a>.</p><p><br></p><p>There is no obligation to continue after your MIAM. If mediation does not feel right for you, that is where it ends.</p><p><br></p><p>If you do want to go ahead, we contact your ex-partner and invite them to attend their own MIAM.</p><p><br></p><p>Your first session together, and how the £500 voucher works</p><p><br></p><p>If you are discussing child arrangements, the government provides a £500 mediation voucher. That is £250 towards your costs and £250 towards your ex-partner’s. We apply for it on your behalf once you have both completed a MIAM and mediation has been assessed as suitable. It is not a cash payment and it does not come to you, it goes straight against the cost of your sessions.</p><p><br></p><p>Standard voucher sessions are covered entirely by the government voucher.∗</p><p><br></p><p>∗Out of hours appointments incur additional fees, as detailed on our <a href=\"{{link_fees}}\">fees page</a>.</p><p><br></p><p>This is the session we book for everyone using the voucher, and we give it two hours rather than 90 minutes on purpose. It is the session that does the most work. You get through far more in one sitting, you are less likely to need an extra session later to finish the same conversation, and most people reach agreement sooner and spend less overall.</p><p><br></p><p>If either of you does not wish to mediate, or mediation is assessed as unsuitable, we can provide the mediator-signed court form enabling a court application.</p><p><br></p><p>Payment</p><p><br></p><p>Fees are payable in advance. Your appointment is confirmed only once payment has been received.</p><p>Payment must reach us at least 72 hours before the appointment, or the held slot is released as per our <a href=\"{{link_terms}}\">terms of use</a>.</p><p>Our current fees are always on our <a href=\"{{link_fees}}\">fees page</a>.</p><p><br></p><p>Next steps</p><p><br></p><p>We have the following appointments available.</p><p><br></p><p>{{date_1}}: {{times}}</p><p><br></p><p>{{date_2}}: {{times}}</p><p><br></p><p>{{date_3}}: {{times}}</p><p><br></p><p>I will send the invoice now. Simply reply with your availability to get booked in, or call us on 0333 240 8038 if you would like more information.</p><p><br></p><p>There is more about how mediation works, what it costs and how we support families on our <a href=\"{{link_mediation}}\">website</a>, and our <a href=\"{{link_faq}}\">FAQs</a> answer the questions we are asked most.</p>",
    "guidance": "Any new enquiry: website form, phone, email or Calendly. Personalise the opening line.",
