    "subject": "The £500 family mediation voucher, explained [{{case_ref}}]",
    "bodyHtml": "<p>Hello {{client_first_name}},</p><p><br></p><p>Thank you for your question about the government voucher. It is a common one, so let me set it out clearly.</p><p><br></p><p>The Family Mediation Voucher Scheme provides up to £500 per case, not per person, towards the cost of mediation sessions.</p><p>It does not cover the MIAM, and it is not a cash payment to you. It is applied directly against your session fees, £250 on your account and £250 on your ex-partner&#x27;s.</p><p>It is available where there is a child under 18 involved in the arrangements being discussed, and where neither of you has claimed before.</p><p>It is not <a href=\"{{link_legalaid}}\">legal aid</a> and is not means tested. You can check legal aid eligibility <a href=\"{{link_legalaid_check}}\">here</a>.</p><p>We apply on your behalf after both of you have attended a MIAM and mediation has been assessed as suitable, so there is nothing for you to do.</p><p>It does not cover missed or late-cancelled sessions.</p><p><br></p><p>Standard voucher sessions are covered entirely by the government voucher.∗ We give it two hours rather than 90 minutes on purpose. It is the session that does the most work, and most people reach agreement sooner and spend less overall.</p><p><br></p><p>∗Out of hours appointments incur additional fees, as detailed on our <a href=\"{{link_fees}}\">fees page</a>.</p><p><br></p><p>The government&#x27;s own guidance is <a href=\"{{link_gov_voucher}}\">here</a>, and our own explainer is on our <a href=\"{{link_voucher}}\">voucher scheme page</a>. Anything above the voucher amount is invoiced as normal, and our current fees are on our <a href=\"{{link_fees}}\">fees page</a>.</p><p><br></p><p>Do come back to me if anything is still unclear.</p>",
